WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:07.140
Movies are a big-money industry, with millions
invested over months, or even years, of production.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:11.830
The slightest alteration can often have a
ripple effect that causes a film's budget

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:13.480
to quickly skyrocket.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.189
Here's a look at movies that lost tons of
money from last-minute changes.

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:22.750
Director Ridley Scott decided to completely
wipe Kevin Spacey from his fim All the Money

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:27.819
in the World after the movie was already wrapped,
following extensive accusations of sexual

00:00:27.819 --> 00:00:29.739
misconduct against Spacey.

00:00:29.739 --> 00:00:35.220
The cost was pre-estimated at around $10 million,
which was a quarter of the film's original

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.620
$40 million budget.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.910
Within 36 hours of making the call to pull
Spacey, Christopher Plummer was announced

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:46.470
as his replacement and 200 new shots were
filmed in only nine days.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:50.890
Reshoot costs aside, there were additional
marketing expenses, including the early push

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:53.440
for Spacey's Oscar nomination that was canned.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.680
The film was pulled from the AFI Fest, which
likely cost Sony six or seven figures.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:03.550
Fortunately, Plummer more than picked up the
slack, becoming the oldest actor to be nominated

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:04.550
for an Oscar.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:10.420
The first Toy Story was a touching animated
movie that helped make Pixar a filmmaking

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:11.460
juggernaut.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:17.000
Disney and Pixar originally planned to release
Toy Story 2 as a 60-minute, direct-to-video

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.920
release, much like the studio had done with
sequels to Aladdin, Cinderella, The Lion King,

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.229
Mulan and even Hunchback of Notre Dame.

00:01:24.229 --> 00:01:27.979
Disney simply didn't do big-screen sequels...Yet.

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:31.969
At a screening with Disney executives, they
decided it was simply too good for the home

00:01:31.969 --> 00:01:32.999
video market.

00:01:32.999 --> 00:01:37.039
They opted to change gears and release it
in theaters as a 90-minute feature.

00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:41.580
With such news, you'd think the production
crew would be ecstatic, however, Pixar's John

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.039
Lasseter wasn't quite so happy.

00:01:44.039 --> 00:01:47.890
He took over the project and rewrote the entire
plot in two days.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:52.270
With an estimated budget of $90 million for
the entire movie, one can assume that about

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:57.049
half that amount was used in the nine-month-long
fire drill that took place after the previous

00:01:57.049 --> 00:01:58.459
yearlong production.

00:01:58.459 --> 00:02:03.030
Work on character creation and key environments
was already done, but there were also several

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.969
notable additions — including the character
of Jessie, who was added in the rewrite.

00:02:07.969 --> 00:02:12.110
Decades ago, when the internet was in its
infancy, a draft of the Scream 2 script was

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:14.570
uploaded to the web during the film's production.

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:20.450
This sent director Wes Craven, the production
team, and Miramax into a panic.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:25.220
Fearing a spoiled ending would ruin the movie,
they quickly decided to rewrite and reshoot

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:26.910
a lot of the film.

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:31.710
It set production back by months, with added
measures to prevent the new script from being

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:32.710
copied.

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:37.360
Three different endings were filmed to throw
everyone off, and as a result, audiences got

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:41.660
both a leaked and a surprise ending to this
decent horror classic.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:48.270
Rewrites, reshoots, and an extra ending certainly
contributed a significant chunk to its $24

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:49.320
million production budget.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:56.200
Superman and Superman II were filmed simultaneously
to cut down on production time, but as costs

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:00.720
mounted, Superman II was stopped so producers
could focus on the first one.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.350
After Superman proved to be a success, production
started again on the sequel.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.520
Well, sort of.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.920
There were a few difficulties, including Marlon
Brando wanting more money and Richard Lester

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.860
replacing the film's original director, Richard
Donner.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:17.070
Lester quickly scrapped some footage and essentially
started over, making the movie his own.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:22.700
With a final budget of around $54 million,
most of that was certainly spent on reshoots.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:28.010
It created massive production expenses but
it resulted in two profitable films that critics

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:29.010
actually enjoyed.

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:34.930
Blade Runner is considered a sci-fi classic
that's beloved by millions.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:38.090
Of course, nothing that great is easy to make.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:42.160
After a few test screenings failed to impress
audiences, several last-minute changes were

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.780
made, including the infamous Harrison Ford
voiceover.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.400
"They don't advertise for killers in a newspaper.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:51.400
That was my profession."

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:57.400
The voiceover was so quick, Ford apparently
never got to read it in advance.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.260
It was recorded eight times, and he was fairly
unhappy about doing it.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:06.069
There were also plot changes, and the ending
was tweaked to make it less mysterious and

00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:07.069
more positive.

00:04:07.069 --> 00:04:11.880
At $28 million, the film was at least $7 million
over budget.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:16.840
While adding a voiceover to an entire movie
might not seem like a monumental cost, the

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:21.370
total added expense left Blade Runner just
shy of recouping its money in theaters.

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:26.090
Fortunately for sci-fi fans and the many filmmakers
who've since been influenced by the movie,

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:28.920
cult status awaited on the home video market.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:34.000
35 years after it faded from theaters, it
got a sequel almost as widely acclaimed as

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.000
the original.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.960
One of the saddest stories surrounding a last-minute
movie change involves former Saturday Night

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.960
Live star Chris Farley, who was the original
voice of Shrek.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.680
At the time of his death, Farley had recorded
80 to 90 percent of his lines.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:51.430
Fellow SNL alum Mike Myers was quickly cast
to replace him.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:56.270
At Meyers' request, script changes were made
to better accommodate his brand of humor.

00:04:56.270 --> 00:05:01.240
Myers realized that his initial take on the
big green guy wasn't quite right, so he wanted

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.080
to re-record his lines with a Scottish accent.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.610
At that point, more than a third of the movie
had been animated, meaning mouth movements,

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:12.780
facial expressions and whatever else was in
the scene would need to be redone.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:17.520
The cost was estimated at $4 million to $5
million, which was about 10 percent of the

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:20.270
film's final budget, but it paid off in the
end.

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:25.800
Before Marvel started its own film studio,
along with the massively successful Marvel

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.260
Cinematic Universe, other studios attempted
to turn the publisher's characters into blockbuster

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:31.260
franchises.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.400
The results were often less than spectacular.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:38.350
Case in point: 2003's Daredevil, starring
Ben Affleck as Matt Murdock.

00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:42.389
Blind lawyer by day, secretly superpowered
vigilante by night.

00:05:42.389 --> 00:05:47.730
It was never meant to be a big-budget superhero
movie, but the success of Sam Raimi's Spider-Man

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:49.750
gave the studio other ideas.

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:55.810
What started as a dark look at the repercussions
of violence was toned down for broader appeal.

00:05:55.810 --> 00:06:00.060
With a contractual obligation to make the
movie PG-13, director Mark Steven Johnson

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.740
had to re-edit Daredevil twice.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:07.460
Test audiences apparently wanted more romance
between Daredevil and Elektra, so more quick

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:08.460
cuts were made.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:13.540
And, again because of the success of the effects
spectacle that was Spider-Man, the studio

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:15.300
wanted more visual thrills.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:19.510
Nearly 30 minutes were chopped, including
a subplot involving Coolio.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:24.550
All of the special effects and edits drove
the budget from $50 million to $80 million.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:28.590
Regardless, it all worked out in the end,
as Daredevil not only made enough money to

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:32.740
spawn the Elektra spinoff, but it also led
to something more for Affleck and Elektra

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:35.470
actress Jennifer Garner, who were married
in 2005.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:41.830
In Enemy Mine, Louis Gossett Jr. plays an
alien named Drac, and Dennis Quaid is Earth

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:43.290
pilot Willis Davidge.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:47.750
They're stranded together on a distant planet
after a battle with — wait for it — each

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:48.750
other.

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:52.120
Naturally, they band together to weather the
elements and defeat a common enemy.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:56.550
The studio was unhappy with early shots from
director Richard Loncraine and decided to

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:59.889
halt production after a large portion of shooting
was completed.

00:06:59.889 --> 00:07:05.260
Execs still loved the concept, actors and
story, so director Wolfgang Petersen was brought

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:06.430
in to finish the job.

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:11.169
Or, really, restart the job and then finish
it, because the footage, sets, and makeup

00:07:11.169 --> 00:07:12.500
weren't up to snuff.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:17.500
After the studio-mandated overhaul, Enemy
Mine's original $17 million budget ballooned

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.600
to over $40 million.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:24.290
It never recouped its costs, earning only
$12 million, but it did eventually earn bragging

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:25.900
rights as a cult classic.

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:31.240
Guillermo del Toro's vision for Mimic, which
involved humans possessed by a mutant tree

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:33.550
beetle, ended up falling flat with studio
execs.

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:36.530
Instead, they envisioned giant killer cockroaches.

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:41.510
Add that fundamental disagreement to the list
of other things the studio didn't appreciate,

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:45.100
and the result is what del Toro calls his
"imperfect child."

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:49.509
The experience was so bad, he almost quit
making movies altogether.

00:07:49.509 --> 00:07:54.010
A second unit was reportedly enlisted in case
the studio decided to replace him.

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:58.330
With an estimated budget of $30 million, it
was generally panned by critics and only made

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:00.040
$25 million back.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:05.260
Del Toro later got to return to make a Director's
Cut, eliminating second-unit shots and reinstating

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:07.040
more atmosphere.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:11.759
He eventually went on to direct classics like
Hellboy, Pan's Labyrinth and The Shape of

00:08:11.759 --> 00:08:12.759
Water.

00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:19.390
With a production budget of $300 million,
a slew of iconic superheroes in one movie,

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:25.710
and a ton of talent on and off the screen,
Justice League just had to succeed, right?

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:27.620
Well, not exactly.

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:30.180
And that hefty price tag didn't really help
either.

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:34.370
Part of the cost resulted from a director
swap that became necessary after Zack Snyder

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:36.649
stepped down to deal with a family tragedy.

00:08:36.649 --> 00:08:41.680
Writer/director Joss Whedon came in and reshot
15 to 20 percent of the movie, costing at

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:43.810
least $25 million.

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:49.320
Having to reshoot scenes is expensive enough,
but bringing a cast back in from other projects

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:53.980
can lead to even more problems, such as the
Henry Cavill hairy-upper-lip debacle.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:58.850
The actor, reprising his role as Superman,
had since grown a mustache for Mission: Impossible

00:08:58.850 --> 00:08:59.850
— Fallout.

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:04.600
As a result, Warner Bros. was forced to digitally
remove Cavill's mustache, adding to the already

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:05.850
inflated budget.

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:10.160
All that extra time and money didn't help
Justice League with critics, and though the

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:14.940
movie still made hundreds of millions of dollars
at the box office, the fallout inspired Warner

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:19.350
Bros. to re-examine its approach and retool
its plan for the DC Extended Universe.

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:22.320
You can call this one a mixed blessing.

